🚽 Test case: the logical NOT operator.
🚽
🚽 `!` had no token at all. lang.l's catch-all rule silently discarded any
🚽 unrecognized character, so `!a` lexed as plain `a` and every negation
🚽 evaluated to its own operand: `!L` was L, and `edgy (!done)` ran the
🚽 wrong branch, with no diagnostic anywhere.
🚽
🚽 Covers every operand type, because `!` cannot borrow the caller's
🚽 context type the way `-` can. A chad must be judged in float precision
🚽 (0.5 is true, so !0.5 is L -- truncating to int would call it false),
🚽 and reading a rizz back through a bool is a type-punned load that UBSan
🚽 rejects outright.
rizz takes_int(rizz x) { bussin x + 1; }
chad takes_float(chad x) { bussin x * 2.0; }
smol takes_short(smol x) { bussin x; }

skibidi main {
    cap a = L;
    rizz n = 5;
    rizz z = 0;
    chad f = 0.5;
    gigachad d = 0.0;
    smol s = 3;

    yapping("%b %b", !a, !W);
    yapping("%b %b", !n, !z);
    yapping("%b", !f);
    yapping("%b", !d);
    yapping("%b", !s);

    🚽 A truth value, never a pointer: `!p` is the null check it looks like.
    🚽 Reporting pointer level 1 here made callers evaluate it as an address.
    rizz *p = &n;
    yapping("%b", !p);

    🚽 Same precedence as unary minus, as in C: these are (!a) == W,
    🚽 (!a) && (!W), and !(!a) -- not negations of the whole expression.
    yapping("%b %b %b", !a == W, !a && !W, !!a);

    🚽 `!` yields cap, and cap does not silently become rizz in this
    🚽 language (`rizz k = W;` is a type error too). But a context that
    🚽 asks for a number still gets C's 0/1, which is what these three
    🚽 reach: an array index, a switch, and typed parameters.
    rizz arr[2] = {7, 9};
    yapping("%d", arr[!z]);

    ohio (!a) {
        sigma rule 1:
            yapping("switch: one");
            bruh;
        based:
            yapping("switch: default");
    }

    yapping("%d", takes_int(!z));
    yapping("%.1f", takes_float(!z));
    yapping("%d", takes_short(!z));

    🚽 The shape the bug actually broke in real code.
    cap done = L;
    rizz i = 0;
    goon (!done) {
        i = i + 1;
        edgy (i >= 3) { done = W; }
    }
    yapping("%d", i);

    🚽 `!=` must still lex as one token, not `!` followed by `=`.
    edgy (n != z) { yapping("ne ok"); }

    bussin 0;
}
